  <h1>The Decimal Splice</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Five is the hinge. Ten is the inversion. Return belongs to the second passage.</p>
</section>

{% include "partials/editorial-guide.njk" %}

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What the earlier theorem permits</p>
    <h2 id="t-from-unit-interval">π Is Transported from the Unit Interval, Not Manufactured by Ten</h2>
    <p>The earlier paper <a href="/pi-from-zero-and-one/">π from 0 and 1</a> proves a bounded statement. The
      symmetric beta kernel on the unit interval has total measure \(\pi\), and the affine coordinate
      \(u=2x-1\) reveals the upper unit semicircle. Zero and one alone do not generate a transcendental
      number by arithmetic. The continuum, endpoint weighting, Euclidean metric and circular closure do the
      work.</p>
    <p>The decimal splice inherits that caution. Ten does not create another π, and five does not become a
      new universal constant. The decimal construction starts with the already selected angular measure and
      transports it across a larger interval. If \(u=x/10\), then the canonical phase \(\pi u\) becomes
      \(\pi x/10\). This is an affine pullback of the unit law.</p>
    <p>That distinction explains why the relation is simultaneously exact and limited. It is exact that the
      midpoint of an origin-anchored interval of width ten is five and that a half-turn distributed uniformly
      across the interval advances by \(\pi/10\) per unit. It is not established that a physical density
      transition distributes phase uniformly, that the relevant domain is additive rather than logarithmic,
      or that an amplitude survives coherent enough for its sign to be measured.</p>
    <p>The new proposal therefore begins one rung above the unit-interval theorem:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \text{Euclidean closure supplies }\pi;
      \qquad
      \text{decimal normalisation supplies }\frac{\pi}{10}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The first statement belongs to declared geometry. The second belongs to that geometry plus TTI’s
      origin-anchored decimal hierarchy. Neither statement by itself supplies a force, field or quantum state.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The decision</p>
    <h2 id="t-decision">One Splice Reverses the Sign; Two Restore It</h2>
    <p class="lede">The decimal splice is not a choice between inversion and return. It orders them. One
      passage produces a half-turn; the double passage produces the full return.</p>
    <p>Write the phase action of one splice as</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      S=e^{i\pi}=-1.
    \]</p>
    <p>Applied to a phase-bearing state \(\psi\), it gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      S\psi=-\psi,
      \qquad
      S^2\psi=e^{i2\pi}\psi=\psi.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is the smallest exact law that fits the existing skeleton. The framework already names \(1\) as
      the self and \(-1\) as the mirror reached by a half-turn. It already describes the splice as a fork in
      which one branch continues and the other is sent back. It also treats two inversions as the operation
      that restores orientation. The decimal phase now makes those statements one calculation rather than
      three neighbouring images. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">The splice is therefore an orientation-reversing \(\mathbb Z_2\) seam: closed after one
      passage as a route, restored after two passages as a state.</p>
    <p class="muted">The algebra is exact once \(S=-1\) is selected. The selection of this operator as a law
      of nature remains a working hypothesis; no physical carrier has yet been identified.</p>
  </div>
</section>

