<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The normalisation</p>
    <h2 id="t-five-pi-over-ten">Why Five and π/10 Appear Together</h2>
    <p>Take the nested decimal interval \([0,10]\) and map it to the unit interval by \(u=x/10\). If one
      passage across the interval is assigned the canonical half-turn, its phase is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \theta(x)=\pi u=\frac{\pi x}{10}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The associated half-wave can be written in two exactly equivalent ways:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      K_{10}(x)
      =\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi x}{10}\right)
      =\cos\!\left(\frac{\pi}{10}(x-5)\right).
    \]</p>
    <p>The second form exposes the decimal hinge. Five is the reflection centre and \(\pi/10\) converts
      displacement from that centre into phase. Their product is not an approximate numerical resemblance:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac{\pi}{10}\,5=\frac{\pi}{2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>That is why five is quadrature. Ten then completes the half-turn:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac{\pi}{10}\,10=\pi.
    \]</p>
    <p>In geometric language, if ten is a diameter then five is its radius. The upper semicircle has arc
      length \(5\pi\). In spectral language, the same interval carries wavenumber \(\pi/10\). The quantities
      \(5\pi\) and \(\pi/10\) are not rival answers. One measures the scaled arc; the other measures phase
      change per unit of the diameter. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A boundary that matters</p>
    <h2 id="t-nested-not-shell">This Is the Interval from Zero to Ten, Not the Shell from One to Ten</h2>
    <p>The appearance of five depends on the geometry being origin-anchored. The midpoint of \([0,10]\) is
      five, but the midpoint of the literal additive shell \([1,10]\) is \(11/2\). If that shell were the
      intended domain, the corresponding half-wave would be</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      K_{1,10}(x)
      =\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi(x-1)}9\right)
      =\cos\!\left(\frac{\pi}{9}\left(x-\frac{11}{2}\right)\right).
    \]</p>
    <p>A logarithmic decade supplies a third geometry. Mapping \(1\leq x\leq10\) by
      \(u=\log_{10}x\) places its midpoint at \(\sqrt{10}\) and gives phase
      \(\pi\log_{10}x\). Neither alternative singles out five.</p>
    <p>TTI’s reason for using \([0,10]\) must therefore be stated: its decimal densities are nested
      completions measured from the same ground, not disjoint additive shells. The stage at ten contains the
      earlier interval rather than beginning only after it. That is a framework choice consistent with its
      diagrams of nought as the common ground. It is not forced by the numeral ten alone.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">This distinction prevents a useful relation from being inflated into a false theorem.
      Five is exact under origin-anchored decimal completion. Change the domain and the centre changes.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Reflection phase</p>
    <h2 id="t-conjugate-pairs">Decimal Partners Carry Opposite Centred Phases</h2>
    <p>Subtract the phase at the hinge and define</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \delta(x)=\theta(x)-\frac\pi2
      =\frac{\pi}{10}(x-5).
    \]</p>
    <p>Decimal reflection sends \(x\) to \(10-x\), so</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \delta(10-x)=-\delta(x).
    \]</p>
    <p>Symmetric positions therefore carry conjugate centred phases, \(e^{i\delta}\) and
      \(e^{-i\delta}\). If coherent amplitudes are recombined before their magnitude is taken, the symmetric
      and antisymmetric channels are</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      e^{i\delta}+e^{-i\delta}=2\cos\delta,
      \qquad
      e^{i\delta}-e^{-i\delta}=2i\sin\delta.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is a legitimate way for decimal position to account for structured phase differences. It does
      not mean that multiplying a state by a unit phase changes its positive norm. An isolated phase is
      invisible to absolute-square energy; a relative phase becomes observable only when coherent paths are
      compared. If the channels are orthogonal or an environment records which path was taken, the interference
      term is reduced or lost.</p>
    <p>The word <em>entanglement</em> should therefore be reserved. Five and \(\pi/10\) are coupled in a
      deterministic kernel. That is not quantum entanglement. Quantum entanglement would require a specified
      tensor-product state that cannot be factorised, together with a preparation and measurement rule.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

