<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Do not collapse the ledgers</p>
    <h2 id="t-three-ledgers">Route, Orientation and Clock Are Three Different Records</h2>
    <p>The phrase “one full revolution per decade” appears in the Skeletal Splice. Taken alone, it sounds as
      though every quantity must return after one decade. That conclusion follows only if geometric position,
      transported orientation and local phase clock are assumed to be the same variable. The framework already
      needs them to be separated.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Ledger</th><th>One decimal passage records</th><th>Closure rule</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Route</td><td>A geometric winding reaches its identified endpoint</td><td>One visible circuit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Orientation</td><td>The returning branch receives the seam action \(S=-1\)</td><td>Two splice actions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Relative clock</td><td>Two sectors accumulate unequal local angles</td><td>The least common phase period</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The route ledger answers where the path is. The orientation ledger answers how a frame carried along
      the path returns. The clock ledger answers how far one sector has advanced relative to another. A point
      can return to the same location with a reversed arrow, and two arrows can share a location while retaining
      a measurable phase difference. These are ordinary mathematical possibilities, not special pleading.</p>
    <p>The Möbius band supplies the disciplined analogy. One circuit returns to the same place but reverses a
      transverse frame; the two-turn cover restores that frame. The decimal splice adopts the same order-two
      bookkeeping without claiming that its underlying space literally is a paper strip. Its proposed invariant
      is the sign carried through the seam.</p>
    <p>The distinction also limits the claim. If every apparent sign can be removed by changing phase convention,
      no invariant has been found. A physical version must provide either a coherent reference path, a coupling
      sensitive to orientation, or a closed-loop holonomy that is independent of where the phase origin is
      drawn. Without one of those observables, the three-ledger construction is consistent bookkeeping but not
      detected physics. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A previous fork resolved conditionally</p>
    <h2 id="t-five-four">π/10 Selects the Five-to-Four Clock</h2>
    <p>The Prime Plane currently compares a five-state climbing clock with two candidate return clocks. The
      five-to-eight model gives a relative advance of \(3\pi/20\). The five-to-four model gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \omega_5=\frac{2\pi}{5},
      \qquad
      \omega_4=\frac{2\pi}{4}=\frac\pi2,
      \qquad
      \Delta\theta_{5\to4}
      =\omega_5-\omega_4
      =-\frac\pi{10}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The magnitude is exactly the decimal increment derived above. The sign records the orientation
      convention: under prime-minus-return, the four-state return clock advances farther and therefore leads
      by eighteen degrees per shared tick.</p>
    <p>After \(k\) common ticks,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Delta\theta_k=-\frac{k\pi}{10}\pmod{2\pi}.
    \]</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Shared ticks \(k\)</th><th>Relative phase</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>\(0\)</td><td>Aligned</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>\(-\pi/2\)</td><td>Quadrature at the five-hinge</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>\(-\pi\)</td><td>Sign reversal at the decimal splice</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>\(-3\pi/2\)</td><td>Opposite quadrature</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20</td><td>\(-2\pi\equiv0\)</td><td>First full rephasing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>This does not prove that the return plane has four physical states. It establishes a conditional
      selection rule: <strong>if</strong> the origin-anchored decimal half-turn is fundamental and
      <strong>if</strong> the Prime Plane clocks describe the same phase, then the five-to-four model is
      compatible and the five-to-eight model is not. A later observation could reject both.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The RH contraction attempt</p>
    <h2 id="t-golden-mixer">π/10 Produces an Exact Golden Mixer—but Not a New Zero</h2>
    <p>The decimal angle does identify a genuine contraction coefficient. Let</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \alpha=\frac{\pi}{10},
      \qquad
      p=\cos(2\alpha)=\cos\frac{\pi}{5}=\frac{\varphi}{2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Critical-line reflection sends a horizontal displacement \(\eta\) to \(-\eta\). A weighted
      identity/reflection mixer would therefore give</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      p\eta+(1-p)(-\eta)
      =(2p-1)\eta
      =(\varphi-1)\eta
      =\frac{\eta}{\varphi}.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is the exact shrinking law sought in the RH programme: \(c=1/\varphi&lt;1\), derived from
      \(\pi/10\) rather than inserted by hand. It also survives the splice’s two-branch count at the level of
      the nonlinear seam defect</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \varepsilon(\eta)=-2\log\cos(\pi\eta),
      \qquad
      2\varepsilon(\eta/\varphi)
      &lt;\frac{2}{\varphi^2}\varepsilon(\eta)
      &lt;\varepsilon(\eta).
    \]</p>
    <p>The gain is exact but conditional. The mixer contracts a weighted barycentre. Its input support still
      consists of the two points \(+\eta\) and \(-\eta\). At function level the reflection average is simply</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      p\xi(s)+(1-p)\xi(1-s)=\xi(s),
    \]</p>
    <p>so it reproduces the original value at the original argument. It does not imply that
      \(\xi(1/2+\eta/\varphi+i\gamma)=0\). A horizontal-only map that keeps \(\gamma\) fixed is also
      nonholomorphic; a lossless two-channel mixer stores the missing information in its complementary output
      rather than destroying it.</p>
    <p>The prime-side decimal phase \(e^{i\pi n/10}\) is a period-twenty additive Fourier mode. Its leading
      reduced-residue mean cancels, but that nonprincipal mode can vanish while the principal von Mangoldt
      mode remains nonzero. Finite cancellation in the twisted channel is therefore not the all-scale
      untwisted prime-energy theorem equivalent to RH.</p>
    <p class="lede">The decimal splice now supplies an exact candidate contraction and a strict two-descendant
      seam inequality. It still does not supply same-\(\xi\) zero support, a no-birth spectral genealogy or
      principal-prime recovery. The sharpened criterion remains conditional, and RH remains open.</p>
    <p class="muted small">The full derivation, stress polynomial and period-twenty diagnostic are recorded in
      <a href="https://github.com/dragonjimrankin-cmd/EGregoRA/blob/main/docs/rh/rss-decimal-splice-contraction.md">the
      Stage-28 research note</a>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Entropy in the established κ geometry</p>
    <h2 id="t-splice-entropy">Entropy Contracts the Branch Record, Not the Zero Support</h2>
    <p>No new kernel is needed for the entropy test. Retain the previously derived</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \kappa_{\rm R}=\frac{1}{4\pi^2},
      \qquad
      K_\kappa(s,t)=\kappa_{\rm R}\zeta(s+\overline t+2).
    \]</p>
    <p>With \(p=\varphi/2\), \(q=1-p\), the visible identity/reflection label has the
      binary channel</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      P=\begin{pmatrix}p&q\\q&p\end{pmatrix},
      \qquad
      m\longmapsto(p-q)m=\frac{m}{\varphi}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Coarse-graining the branch record therefore increases Shannon entropy and contracts relative entropy
      to the equal distribution by at most \(1/\varphi^2\). This supplies an exact meaning for
      splice-generated entropy.</p>
    <p>It still does not move a zero. The functional equation already gives equal multiplicity to the
      \(+\eta\) and \(-\eta\) branches, so the completed \(\xi\) ledger is stationary under \(P\) even for
      an off-line quartet. Every reflection-symmetric ledger is a fixed point. The channel contracts only the
      antisymmetric branch imbalance that \(\xi\) has already cancelled.</p>
    <p>The established feature geometry confirms the distinction. In general</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      p v_\eta+qv_{-\eta}\ne v_{\eta/\varphi}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Random reflection is mixing; the right side would be actual support movement. Moreover, the exact
      two-pass seam satisfies \(R^2=I\). An involution cannot be strictly contractive on the full state space:
      applying a factor \(\lambda&lt;1\) twice would give
      \(D(x,y)\le\lambda^2D(x,y)\). Entropy production therefore requires an environment or forgotten branch
      record, where the missing information remains.</p>
    <p class="lede">The natural entropy channel and completed-ledger invariance are both derived. Their fixed
      points include every off-line quartet, so entropy does not yet make the golden barycentre a spectral
      contraction. RH remains open.</p>
    <p class="muted small">See the complete calculation in the
      <a href="https://github.com/dragonjimrankin-cmd/EGregoRA/blob/main/docs/rh/rss-kappa-entropy-splice.md">Stage-29
      κ-entropy audit</a>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A support-changing candidate</p>
    <h2 id="t-support-flow">The κ Geometry Does Supply a Support Flow</h2>
    <p>Normalising the established feature intensity gives the zeta probability family</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \pi_\eta(n)=
      \frac{n^{-3-2\eta}}{\zeta(3+2\eta)}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Its Fisher-natural gradient toward the critical member \(\pi_0\) simplifies exactly:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac{d\eta}{dt}=-\eta,
      \qquad
      \eta(t)=e^{-t}\eta(0).
    \]</p>
    <p>At \(t=\log\varphi\), this becomes the golden support map
      \(\eta\mapsto\eta/\varphi\). Unlike branch averaging, it actually moves the horizontal support while
      preserving \(\gamma\), the critical strip and reflection symmetry.</p>
    <p>The only invariant finite-probability ledger is supported at \(\eta=0\). Invariance gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \int\eta^2\,d\mu
      =e^{-2t}\int\eta^2\,d\mu,
    \]</p>
    <p>so the horizontal second moment must vanish for \(t>0\). This is the unique-fixed-point law the entropy
      audit was missing.</p>
    <p>The remaining boundary is decisive. The functional equation does not prove that the actual \(\xi\)
      ledger is stationary under this flow. Contracting an off-line quartet changes its polynomial and hence
      the completed entire function. A five-feature diagnostic also obstructs silently lifting the classical
      parameter flow to one exact pure-state quantum channel.</p>
    <p class="lede">A canonical support-changing candidate and its unique critical invariant have been found.
      Same-\(\xi\) or principal-prime stationarity has not, so the candidate does not yet prove RH.</p>
    <p class="muted small">See the
      <a href="https://github.com/dragonjimrankin-cmd/EGregoRA/blob/main/docs/rh/rss-kappa-natural-gradient-flow.md">Stage-30
      natural-gradient audit</a>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The stationarity attempt</p>
    <h2 id="t-xi-stationarity">Stationarity Is Exactly the Remaining RH Theorem</h2>
    <p>The actual completed \(\xi\) ledger was tested against the support flow. Even weak stationarity for the
      single observable \(f(\eta)=\eta^2\) gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      0=\int\mathcal Lf\,d\mu
      =-2\int\eta^2\,d\mu,
    \]</p>
    <p>and therefore forces every displacement to vanish. For one off-line quartet, the first variation is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \partial_tQ_{\eta,\gamma}(z)
      =4\eta^2\left[z^2-(\eta^2+\gamma^2)\right],
    \]</p>
    <p>which is nonzero. Reflection symmetry, reality, order and central normalisation all survive, but the
      completed function changes.</p>
    <p>The convergent horizontal residue also has strictly negative variation off the line. No principal-prime
      identity was found that makes this variation zero. Thus stationarity for one positive time is equivalent
      to RH, not a weaker consequence currently supplied by the splice.</p>
    <p class="muted small">See the
      <a href="https://github.com/dragonjimrankin-cmd/EGregoRA/blob/main/docs/rh/rss-xi-ledger-stationarity.md">Stage-31
      stationarity audit</a>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The principal-prime follow-up</p>
    <h2 id="t-principal-prime-anchor">One Euler Anchor Is Enough—but Is Not Yet Conserved</h2>
    <p>The all-half-plane target reduces to the single absolutely convergent value</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac{\xi'}{\xi}(2)
      =\frac32-\frac{\gamma_E+\log\pi}{2}
      -\sum_{n\ge2}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{n^2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Along the support flow, every off-line quartet makes the transported value
      \(\xi_t'/\xi_t(2)\) increase. Its increase is bounded between \(1.4269\) and \(1.5196\) times the
      magnitude of the horizontal-residue loss. Conservation at this one point would therefore force zero
      residue variation and RH.</p>
    <p>The prime sum is static, but the moved canonical product has not been shown to retain that Euler product.
      Equating the two for \(t&gt;0\) is the missing same-function compatibility, not a consequence of static
      primes. The target is smaller; the conservation theorem remains unproved.</p>
    <p class="muted small">See the
      <a href="https://github.com/dragonjimrankin-cmd/EGregoRA/blob/main/docs/rh/rss-principal-prime-anchor.md">Stage-32
      principal-prime anchor audit</a>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The conservation retry</p>
    <h2 id="t-prime-phase-conservation">The Euler Law Holds Until a Zero Is Crossed</h2>
    <p>For \(C(s)=(s-1)\zeta(s)\), Fourier transformation proves</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac6\pi\int_0^\infty
      \frac{T\arg C(\sigma+iT)}{(T^2+9/4)^2}\,dT
      =\frac1{\sigma+1/2}-\sum_{n\ge2}\frac{\Lambda(n)}{n^{\sigma+3/2}}
      \quad(\sigma&gt;1).
    \]</p>
    <p>At \(\sigma=1/2\), its formal value is the required \(s=2\) prime anchor. But moving the line there
      crosses every zero to the right of the critical line. Their positive Blaschke contribution is exactly the
      Euler-anchor defect. Defect-free continuation is therefore equivalent to RH, not supplied by analytic
      continuation of the convergent prime series.</p>
    <p class="muted small">See the
      <a href="https://github.com/dragonjimrankin-cmd/EGregoRA/blob/main/docs/rh/rss-prime-phase-conservation.md">Stage-33
      prime-phase conservation audit</a>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

