<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">How the proposal could lose</p>
    <h2 id="t-test">A Phase Law Must Predict More Than Its Diagram</h2>
    <p>To become physics, the decimal splice must name a carrier and an observable before data are inspected.
      A clean test would prepare two coherent paths assigned known splice counts, recombine them and compare
      the measured relative phase with</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Delta\theta_k=-\frac{k\pi}{10}\pmod{2\pi}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The decisive pattern is not merely a non-zero shift. It is the complete sequence: quadrature at five,
      sign reversal at ten and rephasing at twenty, with the same orientation convention throughout. A result
      following \(3\pi/20\) per tick would favour the five-to-eight clock instead. A result controlled by
      path length, wavelength, temperature or an ordinary material retardance would support conventional
      dynamics rather than a universal decimal seam.</p>
    <p>The test must also separate phase from intensity and final-state orientation. A sign can be globally
      invisible for one isolated ray yet observable relative to a coherent reference. A restored orientation
      can coexist with environmental path memory. Reporting one detector count cannot distinguish those
      cases.</p>
    <p class="lede">The theory earns the right to say “decimal phase” only if ten fixes a reproducible
      phase relation that cannot be removed by relabelling the coordinate or absorbed into an ordinary
      propagation model.</p>
  </div>
</section>

